## Proof

**Construction.** For each $r \geq 1$, define the random variable $X_r$ by
$$
X_r = \begin{cases}
-r & \text{with probability } 1 - \dfrac{1}{r^2},\\[6pt]
r^3 - r & \text{with probability } \dfrac{1}{r^2}.
\end{cases}
$$
Let $\{X_r\}$ be independent.

**Zero mean.** We compute
$$
\mathbb{E}[X_r] = (-r)\!\left(1-\frac{1}{r^2}\right) + (r^3-r)\cdot\frac{1}{r^2} = -r+\frac{1}{r}+r-\frac{1}{r}=0.
$$
So every $X_r$ has mean zero.

**Only finitely many "large positive" values occur.** Let $A_r = \{X_r = r^3-r\}$, so $\mathbb{P}(A_r)=1/r^2$. Since
$$
\sum_{r=1}^{\infty}\mathbb{P}(A_r)=\sum_{r=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{r^2}<\infty,
$$
the (first) Borel–Cantelli lemma gives
$$
\mathbb{P}(A_r \text{ i.o.})=0.
$$
Hence, almost surely, there exists a (random) integer $N$ such that for all $r>N$ we have $X_r=-r$.

**The sample average diverges to $-\infty$.** On the event of probability one above, fix the corresponding $N$ and let $C=\sum_{r=1}^{N}X_r$ (a finite random constant). For $n>N$,
$$
\sum_{r=1}^{n}X_r = C+\sum_{r=N+1}^{n}(-r)=C-\sum_{r=N+1}^{n}r=C-\frac{n(n+1)-N(N+1)}{2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{r=1}^{n}X_r=\frac{C}{n}-\frac{n+1}{2}+\frac{N(N+1)}{2n}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;-\infty.
$$
Since this holds on an event of probability one,
$$
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{r=1}^{n}X_r \xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}} -\infty.
$$

Thus $\{X_r:r\geq 1\}$ is a sequence of independent, mean-zero random variables whose sample average converges almost surely to $-\infty$. $\blacksquare$

